<p>Northfield United are through to the Champions League final after one of the most extraordinary semi-finals in the competition's modern history. A 94th-minute strike from substitute Marcus Bell completed a comeback from two goals down and sent the home crowd into raptures.</p>

<p>Trailing 2-0 on the night and 3-1 on aggregate with just over half an hour remaining, United looked dead and buried. What followed was a collapse of remarkable proportions from the visitors and a comeback that will be replayed for years to come.</p>

<h2>First Half: Riverside in Control</h2>
<p>Riverside City arrived with a one-goal aggregate lead and promptly doubled it inside twenty minutes. A sweeping counter-attack finished clinically by Tomas Reyes silenced the home support, and when Liam Ford glanced in a second from a corner on 38 minutes, the tie appeared effectively over.</p>

<p>United's midfield was overrun, their press was disjointed, and the home crowd had begun to turn. The half-time whistle was met with a smattering of boos.</p>

<blockquote>
    "At half-time we didn't talk about tactics. We talked about belief. We told them the game was still there if they were brave enough to take it."
    <br><br><strong>— Northfield United manager, post-match conference</strong>
</blockquote>

<h2>The Turning Point</h2>
<p>Whatever was said in the dressing room worked. United emerged transformed, pushing their full-backs high and committing bodies forward. The breakthrough came on 61 minutes when captain Elias Brandt drove a low shot through a crowded penalty area.</p>

<p>Twelve minutes later, the scores were level on the night. A dubious but ultimately upheld penalty was converted emphatically, and suddenly Riverside were clinging on.</p>

<div class="fact-box">
    <div class="fact-title"><i class="fas fa-chart-line"></i> Match Momentum Swing</div>
    <p>United registered 14 shots in the second half compared to just three in the first — a complete reversal of the opening 45 minutes.</p>
</div>

<h2>Stoppage Time Drama</h2>
<p>With the tie heading to extra time, United won a throw-in deep in Riverside territory. The ball was worked into the box, half-cleared, and fell to Marcus Bell — on the pitch for barely eight minutes — who struck it first time from the edge of the area.</p>

<p>The ball flew past a stranded goalkeeper and into the top corner. The stadium erupted. Riverside's players sank to the turf.</p>

